  "result": "I added `click.KeyValue`. It splits a string at the first `=`, so `a=1` becomes `('a', '1')`. The full test suite passes, including 10 new tests for this type.\n\nHow it behaves:\n- **Errors:** input with no `=` (`ab`), or with an empty key (`=b`, `=`), fails with `'ab' is not a valid key=value pair.` The command exits with code 2, like other bad parameter values.\n- **Values:** a value can contain `=` (`a=b=c` gives `('a', 'b=c')`), and it can be empty (`a=` gives `('a', '')`).\n- **Tuples:** a value that's already a tuple, such as a default, is returned unchanged.\n- **Help text:** the option is shown as `--set KEY=VALUE`.\n- **Multiple pairs:** with `multiple=True`, you can turn the result straight into a dict with `dict(settings)`.\n\nChanges:\n- `src/click/types.py`: the new `KeyValue` class, placed after `UUIDParameterType`.\n- `src/click/__init__.py`: exports it as `click.KeyValue`.\n- `docs/api.md`: added to the Types section.\n- `CHANGES.md`: I started a new \"Version 8.6.0 \u2014 Unreleased\" section, because 8.5.0 is already released. The docstring also says `versionadded:: 8.6`, so change both if you plan a different version.\n- `tests/test_types.py`: tests for valid, invalid and multiple values, plus the help output.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
